== Modules for $p$-groups <sec:p-groups>

For a $p$-group $P$ over a field $k$ of characteristic $p$, we shall see that
there is only one simple module, namely the one dimensional trivial module. But
all the complications of how simple composition factors glue together to form an
arbitrary module are already present in the case of a $p$-group.

#lemma[
  Suppose $P$ is a $p$-group and $k$ is a field of characteristic $p$. Then
  there is only one simple $k P$-module, namely the one dimensional trivial
  module $k$.
] <lem:p-group-simple>

#proof[
  If $M$ is a $k P$-module, we shall show that $P$ has a non-zero fixed subspace
  in its action on $M$. Since the fixed subspace is a submodule, it then follows
  that if $M$ is simple then $P$ acts trivially on $M$ and $M$ is one
  dimensional.

  Choose a non-zero element $m$ of $M$ and consider the (additive) abelian
  subgroup of $M$ generated by the images $g(m)$, $g in P$. This is a finite
  abelian $p$-group which admits the action of $P$. Since all orbits must have
  size a power #source(104)of $p$, the number of fixed points is divisible by
  $p$. Since the zero element is fixed, there must be some other fixed point.
]

Thus the decomposition matrix has only one column, and its entries are the
dimensions of the ordinary irreducible representations. The Cartan matrix has
only one entry, which is the group order.

Since there is only one projective indecomposable $k P$-module, namely the
projective cover of the trivial module, this must be equal to the regular
representation. Thus $k P$ has a unique minimal left ideal, also isomorphic to
the trivial module. It is equal to the last non-zero power of the radical, and
is hence a two sided ideal. We write $Soc(k P)$ for this minimal ideal. Since
the projective indecomposable $k P$-module is also injective, whenever it is a
submodule of another $k P$-module it is a summand. Thus every non-projective
indecomposable $k P$-module has $Soc(k P)$ in its kernel. So studying the
representation theory of $k P$ is almost the same as studying the representation
theory of $k P slash Soc(k P)$.

More generally, every transitive permutation module for $k P$ has a one
dimensional socle, since the sum of the elements being permuted spans the unique
minimal submodule. Thus a transitive permutation module for $k P$ is
automatically indecomposable.

Since every $k P$-module is obtained by gluing together one dimensional
composition factors, it is important to understand $Ext^1_(k P) (k, k)$.

#proposition[
  There is a natural isomorphism
  $ H^1 (P, k) = Ext^1_(k P) (k, k) tilde.equiv Hom(P slash Phi(P), k^+) $
  where $k^+$ denotes the additive group of $k$. Thus if $P slash Phi(P)$ is
  elementary abelian of rank $n$ then $Ext^1_(k P) (k, k)$ is an $n$-dimensional
  vector space over $k$.
] <prop:p-group-ext-one>

#proof[
  An extension
  $ 0 -> k -> M -> k -> 0 $
  of $k P$-modules is the same as a matrix representation of the form
  $ g |-> mat(1, alpha(g); 0, 1) $
  where $alpha : P -> k^+$ is a homomorphism of groups from $P$ to the additive
  group of $k$. The kernel of $alpha$ must contain $Phi(P)$, since $k^+$ is
  abelian of exponent $p$.
]

In fact the same argument shows that for any finite group $G$, any element of
$Ext^1_(k G) (k, k)$ corresponds to a two dimensional representation of $G$ with
$O^p (G)$ in its kernel, and so we have the following.

#proposition(closed: true)[
  For any finite group $G$ we have
  $ H^1 (G, k) tilde.equiv H^1 (G slash O^p (G), k). $
] <prop:first-cohomology-quotient>

#source(105)The next observation about cohomology of $p$-groups is that we can
tell whether a module is projective just by looking at its cohomology.

#proposition[
  Suppose $P$ is a $p$-group and $M$ is a $k P$-module. Then the following are
  equivalent.
  + $M$ is projective
  + $H^n (P, M) = 0$ for some $n > 0$
  + $H^n (P, M) = 0$ for all $n > 0$.
] <prop:p-group-projective-cohomology>

#proof[
  By Corollary~@cor:ext-simple we have
  $ H^n (P, M) = Ext^n_(k P) (k, M) tilde.equiv Hom_(k P) (k, Omega^(-n) M), $
  which is non-zero whenever $Omega^(-n) M$ is non-zero, namely whenever $M$ is
  not projective (remember $k P$ is a self injective algebra).
]

#corollary[
  Suppose $P$ is a $p$-group and $M$ and $N$ are $k P$-modules. Then the
  following are equivalent.
  + $M^* times.o_k N$ is projective
  + $Ext^n_(k P) (M, N) = 0$ for some $n > 0$
  + $Ext^n_(k P) (M, N) = 0$ for all $n > 0$.
] <cor:p-group-ext-vanishing>

#proof[
  By Proposition~@prop:tensor-hom-ext we have
  $
    Ext^n_(k P) (M, N) tilde.equiv Ext^n_(k P) (k, M^* times.o_k N)
    tilde.equiv H^n (P, M^* times.o_k N).
  $
  Now apply the above proposition.
]

#subhead[Jennings' theorem]#idx("Jennings' theorem")#idx(
  "theorem",
  "Jennings'",
) We now give an account of Jennings' theorem [@bib:Jennings1941], which
describes the radical layers of the group algebra of a $p$-group. Our
presentation is closer to the one given in Quillen [@bib:Quillen1968].

Suppose $P$ is a $p$-group of order $p^n$, and $k$ is a field of characteristic
$p$. We define the *dimension subgroups*#idx("dimension", "subgroups")#idx(
  "subgroups",
  "dimension",
) of $P$ to be
$ F_r (P) = {g in P | g - 1 in J^r (k P)} $
where $J^r (k P)$ is the $r$th power of the Jacobson radical of the group
algebra $k P$. Since
$
  & (g h - 1) = (g - 1) + (h - 1) + (g - 1)(h - 1) \
  & (g^(-1) - 1) = -g^(-1) (g - 1), quad (h g h^(-1) - 1) = h(g - 1) h^(-1)
$
$F_r (P)$ is a normal subgroup of $P$. Also since
$
  (g^p - 1) = (g - 1)^p, quad
  ([g, h] - 1) = ((g - 1)(h - 1) - (h - 1)(g - 1)) g^(-1) h^(-1)
$
the $p$th power of an element of $F_r (P)$ is in $F_(p r) (P)$, and the
commutators $[F_r (P), F_s (P)]$ are contained in $F_(r+s) (P)$. Also it is easy
to see that $F_1 (P) = P$ and $F_2 (P) = Phi(P)$, the Frattini subgroup of $P$.

We denote by $Gamma_r (P)$ the lowest central series with the above properties.
Namely we begin with $Gamma_1 (P) = P$, and then $Gamma_r (P)$ is generated by
all commutators $[Gamma_s (P), Gamma_t (P)]$ with $s + t gt.eq r$, and all $p$th
powers of elements in $Gamma_s (P)$ with $p s gt.eq r$. This series is called
the *Jennings series*#idx(
  "series",
  "Jennings",
) of $P$. It is clear #source(106)from the above discussion that
$F_r (P) supset.eq Gamma_r (P)$ for all $r > 0$. We shall prove below that
$F_r (P) = Gamma_r (P)$ for all $r > 0$.

It follows from the definition that each $Gamma_r (P) slash Gamma_(r+1) (P)$ is
an elementary abelian $p$-group, which we regard as a vector space over $FF_p$,
say of dimension $n_r$. We form the associated graded object and tensor with $k$
$
  name("Jen")_* (P) = plus.o.big_(r gt.eq 1)
  k times.o_(FF_p) (Gamma_r (P) slash Gamma_(r+1) (P)).
$
This is a Lie algebra, with the Lie bracket given by commutators. The Jacobi
identity#idx("identity", "Jacobi")#idx("Jacobi identity") follows from Philip
Hall's identity#idx("Hall's identity")#idx("identity", "Philip Hall's")
$
  (lconj(y, [x, [y^(-1), z]])) (lconj(z, [y, [z^(-1), x]]))
  (lconj(x, [z, [x^(-1), y]])) = 1.
$
This Lie algebra also comes equipped with a $p$th power operation, the $p$th
power of an element of degree $r$ being an element in degree $p r$. Such an
object is called a *$p$-restricted Lie algebra*#idx(
  "algebra",
  "restricted Lie",
)#idx("Lie algebra, restricted")#idx("restricted Lie algebra") (it is not
necessary for our purposes to know the exact axioms).

We define the restricted *universal enveloping algebra*#idx(
  "enveloping algebra, universal",
)#idx("universal", "enveloping algebra") $cal(U) name("Jen")_* (P)$#idx(
  "$cal(U) \"Jen\"_* (P)$",
) as follows. It is the (associative) graded algebra#idx(
  "graded",
  "algebra",
)#idx("algebra", "graded") over $k$ generated by the graded vector space
$name("Jen")_* (P)$, subject to the relations that $x y - y x$ is equal to the
commutator $[x, y]$, and the $p$th power $x^p$ agrees with the $p$th power
operation defined above. By definition, the identity element of
$cal(U) name("Jen")_* (P)$ spans a copy of the field in degree zero.

We now filter $k P$ by the powers of the radical#idx(
  "radical",
  "filtration of $k P$",
), and define the associated graded object
$ name("gr")_* k P = plus.o.big_(i gt.eq 0) J^i (k P) slash J^(i+1) (k P) $
where $J^0 (k P) = k P$. Since the product of elements in $J^i (k P)$ and
$J^j (k P)$ lies in $J^(i+j) (k P)$, $name("gr")_* k P$ inherits the
multiplication to form an associative graded algebra over $k$.

Since $Gamma_r (P) subset.eq F_r (P)$, we have a vector space homomorphism
$
  phi.alt : name("Jen")_* (P) & -> name("gr")_* k P \
                    macron(g) & |-> macron(g) - 1
$
induced by sending an element $g in Gamma_r (P)$ to $(g - 1) in J^r (k P)$. This
has the property that the Lie bracket $[macron(g), macron(h)]$ goes to
$phi.alt(macron(g)) phi.alt(macron(h)) - phi.alt(macron(h)) phi.alt(macron(g))$,
and $overline(g^p)$ goes to $phi.alt(macron(g))^p$. So by the definition of the
restricted universal enveloping algebra, the map $phi.alt$ extends to an algebra
homomorphism
$ psi : cal(U) name("Jen")_* (P) -> name("gr")_* k P. $

Since $name("Jen")_1 (P) = Gamma_1 (P) slash Gamma_2 (P) = P slash Phi(P)$, the
map $psi$ is an isomorphism in degree one. Now $name("gr")_* k P$ is generated
by elements of degree one, and so $psi$ is surjective. In fact, it is an
isomorphism, as we now show by counting dimensions. This dimension count also
gives us the dimensions of the radical layers of $k P$.

#source(107)To count dimensions, we give a basis of $cal(U) name("Jen")_* (P)$,
called the Poincaré–Birkhoff–Witt basis#idx(
  "basis, Poincaré–Birkhoff–Witt",
)#idx("Poincaré", "–Birkhoff–Witt basis"). First, we choose a basis
$x_1, dots, x_n$ of $name("Jen")_* (P)$, in such a way that the first $n_1$
vectors form a basis of $name("Jen")_1 (P)$, the next $n_2$ vectors form a basis
of $name("Jen")_2 (P)$, and so on. It may happen that some of these are the zero
space, but this does not worry us. We have $n_r = dim_k name("Jen")_r (P)$,
$n = sum n_r$, and $|P| = p^n$. We claim that the elements
$x_1^(alpha_1) dots x_n^(alpha_n)$ with $0 lt.eq alpha_j < p$ span
$cal(U) name("Jen")_* (P)$ as a vector space. This is clear, because the
generators in $name("Jen")_* (P)$ are certainly in this span, and because of the
commutator and $p$th power formulas, a product of such elements is again a
linear combination of such elements.

This proves that the dimension of $cal(U) name("Jen")_* (P)$ is at most $p^n$.
But we have a surjective map
$psi : cal(U) name("Jen")_* (P) -> name("gr")_* k P$, and the dimension of
$name("gr")_* k P$ is $p^n$. This proves that the above elements are linearly
independent, and that $psi$ is an isomorphism. Moreover, an element of
$Gamma_r (P)$ not in $Gamma_(r+1) (P)$ gives rise to a non-zero element of
$name("Jen")_r (P)$ and hence has non-zero image in $name("gr")_r (k P)$. It
follows that $Gamma_r (P) = F_r (P)$.

Finally the above basis allows us to give the dimension of $name("gr")_r k P$.
This is easiest to write down in terms of Poincaré series#idx(
  "Poincaré",
  "series",
).
$
  sum_(i gt.eq 0) t^i dim_k (J^i k P slash J^(i+1) k P)
  = product_r (1 + t^r + dots.c + t^((p-1) r))^(n_r)
  = product_r scaled(((1 - t^(p r))/(1 - t^r)))^(n_r)
$
We summarise what we have proved in the following theorem.

#theorem(title: [Jennings, Quillen], closed: true)[
  Suppose that $P$ is a finite $p$-group. Then the dimension subgroups $F_r (P)$
  are equal to the Jennings subgroups $Gamma_r (P)$. The map
  $ psi : cal(U) name("Jen")_* (P) -> name("gr")_* k P $
  defined above is an isomorphism. In particular, the Poincaré series of the
  radical layers#idx("radical", "layers")#idx("layer", "radical") of $k P$ is
  given by
  $
    sum_(i gt.eq 0) t^i dim_k (J^i k P slash J^(i+1) k P)
    = product_r scaled(((1 - t^(p r))/(1 - t^r)))^(n_r)
  $
  where $|Gamma_r (P) slash Gamma_(r+1) (P)| = p^(n_r)$. Thus the radical length
  of $k P$ is
  $ 1 + sum_r (p - 1) r n_r. $
] <th:jennings>

#corollary[
  The radical layers of $k P$ are equal to the socle layers.
] <cor:radical-socle-layers>

#proof[
  The Poincaré series given above is symmetric. Namely, if we set
  $ l = 1 + sum_r (p - 1) r n_r, $
  the radical length of $k P$, then the above formula shows that
  $
    dim_k (J^i k P slash J^(i+1) k P) = dim_k (J^(l-i-1) k P slash J^(l-i) k P).
  $
  #source(108)By Proposition~@prop:group-algebra-symmetric, $k P$ is self-dual
  as a $k P$-module, so that
  $
    dim_k (J^i k P slash J^(i+1) k P)
    = dim_k (Soc^(i+1) k P slash Soc^i k P).
  $
  Since $Soc^i k P supset.eq J^(l-i) k P$, it follows by induction that we have
  equality.
]
